**Proof.** Recall that the boundary of $C$ is $\partial C = \overline{C}\setminus \operatorname{int}(C)$. Since $x$ is a boundary point of $C$, we have $x\in\partial C$, hence $x\notin\operatorname{int}(C)$.

Suppose, for contradiction, that there exists a neighborhood $U$ of $x$ with $x\in U\subset C$. By definition of neighborhood, there is an open set $O$ with $x\in O\subset U$. Then
$$x\in O\subset U\subset C,$$
so $O$ is an open set containing $x$ and contained in $C$. This means $x\in\operatorname{int}(C)$, contradicting $x\in\partial C$.

Therefore no such neighborhood exists. $\blacksquare$

